#: wraithguard/gui/conflicts.py:1458
msgid "Set tes3conv..."
msgstr ""

#: wraithguard/gui/conflicts.py:1524
msgid "Field comparison for the selected record - RED = a plugin's edit is discarded · AMBER = overridden but nothing lost · ★ = your custom mod · last column wins · double-click a field for the full value:"
msgstr ""

#: wraithguard/gui/conflicts.py:1551
msgid "Field-by-field diff of the selected record. Red = the plugins disagree; the last one in the load order wins.\n\nDouble-click any row for the full value, one tab per plugin. Fields stored as binary are decoded rather than shown raw, so an edit reads as a change instead of a wall of base64:\n  • bytecode -- disassembled to named script instructions. Spans the disassembler cannot decode are printed as offset/hex/ASCII rather than guessed at, and a 'decoded: N%' header says how much was understood.\n  • variables -- the script's local variable names, in declaration order.\n  • landscape (vertex heights, normals, colors, textures, world map) -- decoded to one terrain row per line, so the diff shows which rows of the cell moved. Heights are reconstructed to absolute world units.\n  • path grid connections -- decoded to a per-point adjacency list (point -> its neighbours), so you can see which nodes were rewired."
msgstr ""

#: wraithguard/gui/conflicts.py:1580
msgid "Dump tes3conv JSON..."
msgstr ""

#: wraithguard/gui/conflicts.py:1584
msgid "Conflict map (direct)..."
msgstr ""

#: wraithguard/gui/conflicts.py:1589
msgid "Build and open a conflict map directly from the selected conflicts. Shows which mods edit LAND records in each cell, with a breakdown of terrain shape, NPC navigation, and cell record edits."
msgstr ""

#: wraithguard/gui/conflicts.py:1596
#: wraithguard/gui/conflicts.py:2704
msgid "Add record to patch..."
msgstr ""

#: wraithguard/gui/conflicts.py:1600
msgid "Merge field..."
msgstr ""

#: wraithguard/gui/conflicts.py:1605
msgid "Take the selected field from a plugin of your choosing and keep the rest of the record as it is now -- for when one mod fixed one thing and another mod fixed something else in the same record."
msgstr ""

#: wraithguard/gui/conflicts.py:1613
msgid "Choose which plugin's version of the selected record should win, and add it to a patch. Nothing is written yet, and no mod is ever modified: the patch is one new plugin that loads last."
msgstr ""

#: wraithguard/gui/conflicts.py:1620
#: wraithguard/gui/patchwin.py:232
msgid "Patch Builder..."
msgstr ""

#: wraithguard/gui/conflicts.py:1625
msgid "Review and edit the records queued so far, then write them as one new plugin. Nothing is written until you say so."
msgstr ""

#: wraithguard/gui/conflicts.py:1630
msgid "Plugin view..."
msgstr ""

#: wraithguard/gui/conflicts.py:1634
msgid "The same scan with the load order's own shape restored: plugin, then kind of record, then record, with the record compared across every plugin that defines it.\n\nA flat list answers 'what conflicts'. This answers 'what does this mod change, and where does it lose', which is the question you are actually asking. The colours fill in on their own once the conflict window's background survey finishes. Read-only."
msgstr ""

#: wraithguard/gui/conflicts.py:1646
msgid "Plugin summary..."
msgstr ""

#: wraithguard/gui/conflicts.py:1651
msgid "Judge every conflict in the list and count the result per mod, rather than per record.\n\nA flat list of conflicts cannot answer the question that decides load order: which of my mods is losing work? This compares each record field by field and counts, for every plugin, how many records it edits and does not get its way on.\n\nReads every conflicting record with tes3conv, so it takes a moment on a large load order. Read-only."
msgstr ""

#: wraithguard/gui/conflicts.py:1721
msgid "Busy reading plugins -- try that again in a moment."
msgstr ""

#: wraithguard/gui/conflicts.py:1769
msgid "Nothing to summarise."
msgstr ""

#: wraithguard/gui/conflicts.py:1775
msgid "Judge every conflict?"
msgstr ""

#: wraithguard/gui/conflicts.py:1776
msgid "This reads every plugin that defines any of these %(count)d records -- once each, not once per record -- and will take a few minutes. The checkboxes and filters above narrow the list first if you would rather summarise part of it.\n\nGo ahead?"
msgstr ""

#: wraithguard/gui/conflicts.py:1791
msgid "Colouring the load order (%(count)d record(s))..."
